<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- Plain http is not an exception in this app, it is the job. Nearly every URL
     it opens is one the *user* chose and nobody here can vouch for the scheme
     of: the torrent engine's own API on 127.0.0.1, another Ventic's read-only
     mirror on a bare LAN address while a cast lasts (cast.rs, port 3231), an
     IPTV channel out of somebody's M3U, and whatever a source resolved a
     release to.

     This was loopback-only, and the two that are not loopback were the two that
     broke. A cast never plays on an Android receiver: the mirror's address is a
     LAN IP literal, Android has no way to express "the local network" here —
     `domain` matches host names, there is no CIDR form — and everything outside
     the list falls to `base-config`. The failure is invisible from both ends.
     The sending device is told the film is fine, because the receiver tests it
     with a TCP connect from Rust, which this policy does not touch; the
     receiving device blocks the *webview's* fetch and ExoPlayer's open, so the
     only symptom is a spinner on a television and an error blaming the link.
     Live TV goes the same way for any channel served over http.

     So: permitted, and narrowed where it can be narrowed instead — the mirror
     is read-only, bound to the LAN for the length of one cast, and gated on a
     pairing code (see cast.rs). `bun run check:cast` holds this file, because
     nothing else can: tightening it compiles, installs, and fails only on
     somebody else's TV. -->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
